A||N||8||DaveL||Temp Guage is making me HOT||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||02-17-2000||07:59 AM||||My temp sending unit (87 2.5 DIS) pegs and stays pegged as long as ignition is on. Also, there is an aftermarket temp guage mounted to the left side of the center console. The original plug to the wiring harness from the original sender is just hanging. I guess I'll have to run these wires from sensor to harness (ECM and plugs}, but are there any other possible areas for shorts in this circuit? Also, anyone else with an 87 SE 2.5 DIS, when you look at the engine compartment from the rear, how many grounds come from the left deck hinge, and where exactly do they go? I have one loose black ground wire attached to nothing. It looks to be long enough to reach the block, but I can't really read wiring diagrams very well. Anyone care to take a shot?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||02-17-2000||07:36 PM||||on 87 dis.....<P>Bat main ground goes to the block. usually a stud on head bolt 9 I think. to dark to look now. Bat sub ground goes to a screw on the body nearly straight down from the + bat terminal.<P>try just pluging in the OEM sender. An open on the gauge may peg it. Don't know for sure, again, to dark to check. someone may have done this to "correct" the gauge pegging and sticking on cranking the motor. The correct fix for that will both protect the factory gauge, enable the idiot light, and eliminate the need for the aftermarket one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||02-17-2000||10:06 PM||||The pegging temp guage is a problem found on all Fieros.  GM wired the temp guage wrong, so it pegs on startup.<P>Heres the Fix:<P><BR>1. Remove the instrument pod back cover and steering column bottom <BR>cover. <BR> <BR>2. In the instrument pod, remove the white or black plug on the <BR>right side (by the tachometer). <BR> <BR>3. Locate the 4th wire (green) from the bottom on the right side <BR>(marked "13"). <BR> <BR>4. Tap into this wire with a 12" long wire, and feed the free end <BR>down towards the steering column. <BR> <BR>5. Measure 11" down the steering cloumn from the ignition switch to <BR>find a black connector on top of the column. <BR> <BR>6. Find 2 green wires running down the right side of the column, and <BR>pull them far enough out to gain access so that you can cut them <BR>(both) halfway between the connector and the harness. <BR> <BR>7. Connect both green wires coming from the connector to the wire <BR>fed from the instrument pod. <BR> <BR>8. Connect the 2 remaining wires coming from the harness to each <BR>other. <BR> <BR>9. Insulate all connections and replace the covers. ||||reg||7
Z||000003||stimpy||02-17-2000||11:14 PM||||Seems like I have a different type of prob with m guage. Yes, it does peg on start-up. But once I let off the key, the needle drops back down and stays to the left side, never moving. The shop that did my rebuild hooked up diagnostic and told me that the sending unit was fine as they were able to get temp reading. Is it possible that my guage is kaput? Will the salvage yard guage I found likely work? Help me Mr. Wizard! ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||02-18-2000||06:32 AM||||Stimpy<BR>Try grounding the wires at the sending unit (on the V6 they are on the head left side trunk side). The idiot light should light and gage should peg, if not it's either gage or wiring.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaveL||02-18-2000||08:36 AM||||To further clarify, when the ignition is turned on, the temp needle goes all the way to hot end of scale and stays there. When I turn off the ignition, it falls back to the left end, not physically sticking. Also when the ignition is turned on without engine starting, the temp idiot light doesn't light at all like all the others. I hope the fix outlined above is the right one because cutting into factory wires makes me nervous. I don't want to pick my peaches before they're good and fuzzed up.As for the ground, I'm not talking about the ground from the battery itself, but rather the two ground leads coming down from the deck lid hinge on the opposite side of the engine compartment. That would be the left side if viewed standing behind the car looking forward. One thicker guage ground goes directly to the block. But there is a smaller guage loose ground from the same hinge that currently goes nowhere. It's long enough to reach the block, but I can't see any reason for it. Does anyone else have these leads, and where does the smaller one go?||||reg||10
Z||000006||DJRice||02-18-2000||09:45 AM||||You mentioned that you have an aftermarket gauge also.  Did you install the gauge or did a previous owner install the gauge?  It could be that the temp sending unit had to be replaced to support the new gauge.  Also, if the aftermarket gauge is not mounted with the rest of them, its possible that the person who mounted the gauge cut a wire between the engine compartment and the gauge pod.  That would cause the temp gauge to "float."<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaveL||02-18-2000||03:13 PM||||Yes a previous owner added the aftermarket guage. This has one wire going to the guage, and the guage has one ground wire connected to a nut under the steering column, and a hot lead that runs over to the fuse box. I have it all unhooked now and am running with no temp guage at all. My guess is the original owner found the factory guage has a short in it so rather than find the short, he just added the new guage. But according to the above thread, this may be a common problem. Doesn't seem as though someone would buy a new car with a temp guage that didn't work. The original plug for the sender is just hanging loose, two wire plug. According to the Helms manual if you ground the green wire, the guage should stay pegged, but the plug isn't even connected and still the guage stays pegged until shutdown.  Dang||||reg||1
Z||000008||daved||02-18-2000||11:02 PM||||Davel, Seems to me that either the gauge is defective, or there is 12Volts staying on the #11 lt. green wire at the pod. If the latter, then it comes from the ignition switch. Pull the pod cover, and disconnect the the LHS (from the drivers position)connector...its the horizontal one. With the engine running, check the voltage (in the plug) at above wire. <BR> Only at crank does the ignition switch send full voltage to the gauge (the famous factory wiring goof), so when in "run", no voltage will be present.  If you see voltage at other than crank, then the ignition switch is defective.<BR>Suggest you check the ground also, marked #4 and is a black wire (though no idea where the common ground S212 is...under the column?)  Good Luck!||||reg||1
